					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/blastyoboots" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(224446); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=224446&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608822579" alt="BlastYoBoots" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=224446)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/blastyoboots" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(224446); return false;" >BlastYoBoots</a></span> <span class="odate time_1231802550 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">12 Jan 2009 23:22</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-354697">
									

<p>It seems a little too benevolent, though fortune cookies are designed that way, anyway. Seconded on cross-testing with humanoid SCPs, but there should also be some limited testing on the following inquiries:</p>
<ul>
<li>Since the fortunes often indicate notable and close events, do the fortunes actually cause them?</li>
<li>Do the fortunes come true if the opener does not read them?</li>
<li>Do the fortunes come true if nobody reads them?</li>
<li>Does SCP-377 exhibit sentience?</li>
</ul>
<p>I'd be willing to construct a battery of tests, though I've never done something like this before.</p>

